menu "CPE Maintenance Configuration"

    config CPE_MAX_REBOOTS
        int "Maximum consecutive controlled reboots before staying in fault mode"
        default 3
        range 0 10
        help
            The boot counter is persisted in NVS and cleared after the device
            has been online for CPE_HEALTHY_CLEAR_SECONDS, so a panic loop can
            never restart forever. 0 disables controlled reboots.

    config CPE_HEALTHY_CLEAR_SECONDS
        int "Uptime required to clear the boot counter (seconds)"
        default 120
        range 10 3600

    config CPE_MAINT_ECM_PERIOD_MIN
        int "Periodic ECM interface rebuild (minutes, 0 = disabled)"
        default 0
        range 0 43200
        help
            Every N minutes the ECM network interface is torn down and rebuilt
            WITHOUT rebooting the ESP32: the SoftAP and the web console stay
            up, the modem is not reset, only the WAN interface is recreated.
            The WAN is unavailable for a few seconds while the DHCP client
            obtains a new lease.

            This clears driver/lwIP/modem-session state that slowly degrades
            over long runs and is the cheapest way to recover a WAN path that
            is "up but not passing traffic". The action is skipped while an
            OTA upload or an unvalidated OTA image is pending and the request
            is executed by the link manager task, never concurrently with its
            own recovery actions.

            This Kconfig value is only the DEFAULT (first boot / factory
            reset): the active value is stored in NVS and can be changed at
            runtime from the management console (/api/system/maintenance).

            0 disables the periodic rebuild.

    config CPE_MAINT_REBOOT_PERIOD_MIN
        int "Periodic device reboot (minutes, 0 = disabled)"
        default 0
        range 0 43200
        help
            Every N minutes the ESP32 reboots after the standard pre-restart
            cleanup (AT+CFUN=1,1 when the AT port is available, then Wi-Fi
            deauthentication). This is the last-resort cure for a firmware or
            modem state that a soft recovery cannot fix. All SoftAP clients
            are disconnected and the 4G link is down for tens of seconds.

            A scheduled reboot is skipped while an OTA upload/image validation
            is in progress and it is counted by a dedicated NVS guard
            (CPE_MAINT_MAX_SCHED_REBOOTS): after that many consecutive
            scheduled reboots that never reached a steady state (the link
            manager left the boot phase) the timer stops rebooting and only
            reports the fault, so a firmware that cannot come back up is not
            rebooted forever. A missing SIM/modem does not count as unhealthy,
            only a device that keeps failing to boot does.

            This Kconfig value is only the DEFAULT (first boot / factory
            reset): the active value is stored in NVS and can be changed at
            runtime from the management console (/api/system/maintenance).

            0 disables the periodic reboot.

    config CPE_MAINT_RESET_MODEM
        bool "Reset the 4G module before a scheduled reboot"
        default y
        help
            Send AT+CFUN=1,1 (best effort, bounded to
            CPE_AT_MODEM_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS) before the scheduled reboot so the
            modem re-enumerates cleanly afterwards. Only applies to the
            scheduled reboot: the periodic ECM rebuild never resets the modem.
            Disable this when the modem drops off the bus during its own reset
            and needs a physical replug.

    config CPE_MAINT_MAX_SCHED_REBOOTS
        int "Consecutive scheduled reboots without a healthy period"
        default 3
        range 1 20
        help
            The count is persisted in NVS and cleared once the device has been
            in a steady state for CPE_HEALTHY_CLEAR_SECONDS ("steady" = the
            link manager left the boot phase, the 4G link itself is not
            required). When the limit is reached the scheduled reboot is frozen
            (and reported in the status error text) instead of rebooting a
            device that cannot boot.

endmenu
